   "path": "sections.context.2.en",
   "before": "Notice what the speech never says: it does not deny that Wu Qi can fight. It attacks the man and the ledger of using him — a flaw in private character, no return home at his mother's death, a small state that cannot afford a reputation for victory. The author infers that the thrust of the speech is to swap one question for another: not can he win, but can we afford him. The source records no reaction from Wu Qi to this suspicion, and this article will not write one for him.",
   "after": "Notice what the speech never says: it does not deny that Wu Qi can fight. It attacks the man and the ledger of using him — a flaw in private character, no return home at his mother's death, a small state that cannot afford a reputation for victory. The author infers that the thrust of the speech is to swap one question for another: not can he win, but can we afford him. (The author's interpretation.) Once dismissed, he heard that Marquis Wen of Wei was worthy and sought to serve him; the source gives no inner reaction, and this article will not supply one.",
